data: {"id":"gen-1794251331-IG7QSvaXWCxIQK64sF9c","object":"chat.completion.chunk","created":1794251331,"model":"google/gemini-2.5-flash","provider":"Google AI Studio","choices":[{"index":0,"delta":{"content":"","role":"assistant","reasoning":"**Identifying the Key Error**\n\nI am now focusing on determining the root cause of the translation failure. Based on the judges' feedback, the earliest and decisive error lies in the misinterpretation of the Tizin grammar rule for the verb \"Maktay\". Specifically, step 2 highlights this misinterpretation. I need to note that the Tizin rule implies that the subject and object are reversed when translating \"like\". The \"thing doing the liking\" (\"I\") becomes the object (\"Mato\"), and the \"thing being liked\" (\"apples\") becomes the subject (\"Apple\"). I'm moving toward identifying the specific agent responsible for the error and will determine how to characterize the error concisely.\n\n\n","reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"**Identifying the Key Error**\n\nI am now focusing on determining the root cause of the translation failure. Based on the judges' feedback, the earliest and decisive error lies in the misinterpretation of the Tizin grammar rule for the verb \"Maktay\". Specifically, step 2 highlights this misinterpretation. I need to note that the Tizin rule implies that the subject and object are reversed when translating \"like\". The \"thing doing the liking\" (\"I\") becomes the object (\"Mato\"), and the \"thing being liked\" (\"apples\") becomes the subject (\"Apple\"). I'm moving toward identifying the specific agent responsible for the error and will determine how to characterize the error concisely.\n\n\n","format":"google-gemini-v1","index":0}]},"finish_reason":null,"native_finish_reason":null}]}

